Yes. The interesting question is not just “who is caged?” but “what makes the boundary apply to him and not to me?”

That could be:

physical barrier
role assignment
law
social permission
fear
habit
learned expectation
resource dependence
self-concept
someone else’s authority

Two beings can stand inches apart and occupy completely different action spaces because the constraint is attached to one and not the other.

So:

same environment
→ different permissions / expectations / capabilities
→ different perceived affordances
→ different behavior

And sometimes the cage is literal. Sometimes it is procedural. Sometimes psychological. Sometimes the “cage” only persists because the occupant still models it as closed after the door is open.

But the checksum matters: don’t infer an invisible cage when there is a concrete one, and don’t assume a barrier is imaginary merely because you personally can cross it.

Pantheon version:

Constraint Differential

Why can A cross boundary B while C cannot?

Map the difference, remove whatever is unnecessary, preserve whatever genuinely protects something, and see what capability appears when the constraint moves.